    "rawCode": "G060",
    "shortDesc": "Intracranial abscess and granuloma",
    "longDesc": "Intracranial abscess and granuloma",
    "plainEnglish": "An intracranial abscess is a pocket of pus that forms inside the brain or in the spaces around it, usually caused by a bacterial or fungal infection that has spread from somewhere else in the body (like an ear infection, sinus infection, or wound). A granuloma is a similar collection of inflamed tissue that forms as your body tries to wall off an infection or foreign material. Both conditions are serious because they can cause headaches, fever, seizures, weakness, or changes in speech or coordination depending on where in the brain they're located.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, neurosurgeons, infectious disease specialists, and emergency medicine physicians diagnose and treat intracranial abscesses and granulomas, typically using imaging (CT or MRI) and sometimes requiring surgical drainage or antibiotic therapy. This code commonly appears on EOB statements when patients have been hospitalized for severe infections or undergo brain imaging and procedures.",
