    "longDesc": "Intraspinal abscess and granuloma",
    "plainEnglish": "An intraspinal abscess and granuloma is a localized infection or inflammatory pocket within the spinal cord or the space around it. This infection can develop from bacteria spreading from nearby tissues, spinal procedures, or other sources, and causes the body to form a collection of pus (abscess) or a ball of inflamed tissue (granuloma). Patients typically experience back pain, weakness, numbness, or loss of function in the legs, depending on where the infection is located and how much pressure it puts on the spinal cord.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, neurosurgeons, and infectious disease specialists use this code when diagnosing and treating spinal cord infections or inflammatory conditions. It commonly appears on patient EOBs when someone has had imaging (MRI), spinal procedures, or hospitalization related to a suspected or confirmed spinal infection.",
