    "plainEnglish": "Huntington's disease is a rare genetic disorder where a person inherits a faulty gene that causes progressive damage to nerve cells in the brain over time. This leads to involuntary movements (like jerking or twitching), emotional problems such as depression or mood changes, and declining mental abilities including memory loss and difficulty concentrating. Symptoms typically appear in mid-adulthood and slowly worsen over 10-20 years, eventually affecting a person's ability to work, care for themselves, and live independently.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and geneticists primarily use this code when diagnosing and managing Huntington's disease in patients, often in specialized movement disorder or neurodegenerative disease clinics. This code appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing genetic testing, brain imaging, medication management, or specialized neurological care related to Huntington's disease.",
