    "shortDesc": "Other early-onset cerebellar ataxia",
    "longDesc": "Other early-onset cerebellar ataxia",
    "plainEnglish": "Early-onset cerebellar ataxia is a rare neurological condition where the cerebellum (the part of the brain that controls balance and coordination) doesn't develop or function normally, causing problems with walking, balance, and fine motor movements that begin in childhood or early adulthood. People with this condition typically experience progressive difficulty with coordination, speech problems, and unsteady movements, though the specific symptoms and severity vary depending on the underlying cause. Unlike some other types of ataxia that result from known genetic mutations, this code describes cases where the cerebellar problem starts early in life but doesn't fit into other more specific diagnostic categories.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and pediatric neurologists use this code when diagnosing children or young adults with progressive coordination and balance problems related to cerebellar dysfunction. This code may appear on EOB statements when genetic testing, brain imaging, or specialist consultations are ordered to investigate the cause of ataxia, though it's relatively uncommon in general practice since these conditions are rare.",
