    "shortDesc": "Neuroleptic induced parkinsonism",
    "longDesc": "Neuroleptic induced parkinsonism",
    "plainEnglish": "Neuroleptic-induced parkinsonism is a side effect that develops when someone takes certain psychiatric medications (called neuroleptics or antipsychotics) that interfere with dopamine in the brain, causing symptoms similar to Parkinson's disease—such as tremors, muscle stiffness, and slowness of movement. This is a temporary condition that typically improves when the medication is adjusted or stopped, unlike true Parkinson's disease which is a permanent neurological disorder. Patients may notice shakiness in their hands, difficulty moving smoothly, or a stiff, shuffling gait within days or weeks of starting or increasing these medications.",
    "whoUses": "Psychiatrists, neurologists, and primary care physicians use this code when documenting medication side effects in patients taking antipsychotic medications. It commonly appears on EOB statements when patients seek care specifically for managing or treating medication-related movement problems.",
