    "plainEnglish": "Postencephalitic parkinsonism is a movement disorder that develops after someone recovers from encephalitis (brain inflammation, usually caused by a viral infection). The condition causes symptoms similar to Parkinson's disease—such as tremors, stiffness, and slow movements—but it occurs as a long-term consequence of the earlier brain infection rather than from the typical degeneration seen in classic Parkinson's disease. Patients may notice these movement problems months or even years after their initial encephalitis infection has healed.",
