    "shortDesc": "Drug-induced chorea",
    "longDesc": "Drug-induced chorea",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis means you're experiencing involuntary, jerky, or dance-like movements of your body caused by a medication you're taking. Chorea is a movement disorder where your muscles contract unpredictably, making it difficult to control your limbs or body position; certain drugs—especially psychiatric medications like antipsychotics or some Parkinson's medications—can trigger this side effect. You might notice restless, flowing movements that happen without your control, and they typically improve once the offending medication is stopped or adjusted.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, psychiatrists, and primary care physicians use this code when patients develop involuntary movements as a medication side effect. It commonly appears on EOBs for patients on antipsychotics, dopamine agonists, or other medications known to cause movement disorders, and often leads to follow-up neurology visits to adjust treatment.",
