    "plainEnglish": "Pick's disease is a rare type of dementia that causes progressive damage to the front and side portions of the brain, leading to changes in personality, behavior, and language abilities early on—often before memory loss becomes severe. This condition causes brain cells in those areas to shrink and die, resulting in symptoms like difficulty speaking, inappropriate social behavior, apathy, and eventual cognitive decline. It is a form of frontotemporal dementia and typically develops in people in their 40s through 60s.",
