    "rawCode": "G3180",
    "shortDesc": "Leukodystrophy, unspecified",
    "longDesc": "Leukodystrophy, unspecified",
    "plainEnglish": "Leukodystrophy is a rare inherited disorder that damages the white matter in your brain and spinal cord—the insulating covering around nerve fibers that allows them to communicate properly. This progressive condition interferes with nerve signals, typically causing gradual symptoms like loss of coordination, muscle weakness, vision problems, or cognitive decline, depending on which areas of the brain are affected. When doctors use the 'unspecified' code, it means they've identified that you have a leukodystrophy but haven't yet pinpointed which specific type you have, often because genetic testing or additional evaluation is still pending.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and pediatric neurologists primarily use this code when evaluating patients with progressive neurological symptoms suggestive of white matter disease. It commonly appears during the diagnostic workup phase before a specific leukodystrophy subtype (like metachromatic leukodystrophy or Krabbe disease) is identified, and may show up on EOB statements as testing and specialist evaluations are ordered.",
