    "rawCode": "G3182",
    "shortDesc": "Leigh's disease",
    "longDesc": "Leigh's disease",
    "plainEnglish": "Leigh's disease is a rare, inherited neurological disorder that affects how your body's cells produce energy, particularly in the brain and nervous system. This progressive condition typically begins in infancy or early childhood and causes developmental delays, muscle weakness, loss of coordination, and sometimes seizures or breathing problems as nerve cells gradually deteriorate due to abnormal mitochondrial function. The condition is caused by mutations in genes that control mitochondria (the energy-producing parts of cells), meaning it's passed down through families and currently has no cure, though treatments focus on managing symptoms.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, pediatric neurologists, and metabolic geneticists use this code when diagnosing and managing patients with Leigh's disease. It commonly appears in medical records for children with this condition but is uncommon on patient EOB statements outside of specialized pediatric neurology centers, as the diagnosis requires genetic testing and specialized imaging confirmation.",
