    "code": "G31.87",
    "rawCode": "G3187",
    "shortDesc": "Primary progressive apraxia of speech",
    "longDesc": "Primary progressive apraxia of speech",
    "plainEnglish": "Primary progressive apraxia of speech is a rare neurological condition where someone gradually loses the ability to plan and coordinate the movements needed to speak clearly, even though they understand what they want to say and their vocal cords work properly. The condition worsens over time, causing speech to become increasingly difficult, slurred, or hard to understand—similar to having a disconnect between the brain's language center and the muscles that control speech. Patients typically notice their speech becoming slower or more effortful, with difficulty pronouncing words correctly, even though their thinking and comprehension remain relatively intact.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and speech-language pathologists use this code when diagnosing and managing progressive speech disorders, typically in cases of neurodegenerative disease. It appears on patient EOBs when billing for specialty neurology evaluations, speech therapy, or diagnostic testing related to primary progressive apraxia of speech.",
