    "rawCode": "G319",
    "shortDesc": "Degenerative disease of nervous system, unspecified",
    "longDesc": "Degenerative disease of nervous system, unspecified",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes a condition where parts of your nervous system are gradually breaking down or deteriorating, but the specific type of degeneration hasn't been identified or documented. Your nerves and brain tissue are slowly losing function over time, which can cause problems with movement, thinking, balance, or other body functions depending on which areas are affected. Without more testing or a clearer diagnosis, your doctor is using this general category to capture that something is wrong with your nervous system but the exact disease isn't pinpointed yet.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, geriatricians, and primary care physicians use this code when a patient shows signs of progressive neurological decline but the specific degenerative disease (like Parkinson's, Alzheimer's, or multiple system atrophy) hasn't been confirmed. It commonly appears on EOB statements as a temporary or provisional diagnosis while doctors are still working through testing and specialist evaluations to narrow down the exact condition.",
