    "shortDesc": "Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis",
    "longDesc": "Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis",
    "plainEnglish": "Relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS) is the most common form of MS, where your immune system periodically attacks the protective coating around nerve fibers in your brain and spinal cord. This causes periods of new or worsening symptoms (called relapses or flare-ups) followed by periods where symptoms improve or disappear (remissions), though some nerve damage may persist over time. People with RRMS might experience episodes of fatigue, vision problems, numbness, or weakness that come and go unpredictably.",
