    "plainEnglish": "This code describes a sudden, widespread attack on the protective covering (myelin) surrounding nerve fibers in your brain and spinal cord, but the doctors haven't yet identified the specific type or underlying cause. When myelin is damaged, nerve signals can't travel properly, leading to symptoms like weakness, numbness, vision problems, or difficulty with coordination that develop rapidly over days or weeks. This is a serious condition that requires urgent medical evaluation and treatment to prevent permanent nerve damage.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and hospitalists use this code when patients present with acute neurological symptoms suggestive of demyelinating disease (such as optic neuritis, transverse myelitis, or acute disseminated encephalomyelitis), typically in hospital or emergency settings where imaging and testing are underway. This code is less commonly seen on patient EOBs because it's usually a temporary admission diagnosis that gets refined to a more specific demyelinating condition once test results return.",
