    "longDesc": "Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein antibody disease",
    "plainEnglish": "Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG) antibody disease is a rare autoimmune condition where your immune system mistakenly attacks a protein found on the protective coating (myelin) around nerve fibers in your brain and spinal cord. This attack causes inflammation and damage to the nerve fibers, leading to symptoms like vision loss, weakness, numbness, or balance problems that can come and go. It's similar to multiple sclerosis but is a distinct condition identified by the presence of specific antibodies against the MOG protein.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and neuroimmunologists primarily diagnose and manage this condition, often after specialized blood tests confirm the presence of MOG antibodies. This code appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing diagnostic testing (MRI, lumbar puncture, blood work) or receiving treatment, though it is relatively uncommon compared to more prevalent neurological diagnoses.",
    "billable": true,
    "isNew": false,
    "isRevised": false,
    "chapterId": 6,
