    "rawCode": "G40A01",
    "shortDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, not intractable, w stat epi",
    "longDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, not intractable, with status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "Absence epilepsy is a type of seizure disorder where you experience brief episodes of staring and loss of awareness that last a few seconds, during which you're unresponsive but don't fall down or jerk uncontrollably. In this case, you're currently in or have recently experienced status epilepticus, which means the seizures are happening repeatedly or continuously without full recovery between episodes—a serious condition that requires immediate medical attention. The 'not intractable' part means your seizures can still be controlled with medication, which is encouraging for long-term management.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and epilepsy specialists use this code when diagnosing and treating absence seizure patients, particularly those experiencing the medical emergency of status epilepticus. This code commonly appears on EOB statements for patients hospitalized or treated in emergency departments for prolonged or recurring absence seizures, as well as for ongoing epilepsy management visits.",
