    "shortDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, not intractable, w/o stat epi",
    "longDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, not intractable, without status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "Absence epilepsy is a type of seizure disorder where a person experiences brief episodes of sudden unconsciousness—typically lasting only a few seconds—during which they stare blankly and don't respond to their surroundings. These seizures are not \"intractable,\" meaning they can be controlled with medication rather than being resistant to treatment. You may experience dozens of these episodes throughout the day, and you might not even realize they're happening, but they can interfere with attention, learning, and daily activities.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and primary care physicians use this code when diagnosing and managing patients with absence seizures that respond well to anti-epileptic medications. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients receiving ongoing seizure management, EEG testing, and medication monitoring for this specific epilepsy syndrome.",
