    "rawCode": "G40A11",
    "shortDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, intractable, w stat epi",
    "longDesc": "Absence epileptic syndrome, intractable, with status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "Absence epilepsy is a type of seizure disorder where you experience frequent, brief periods of unconsciousness—typically lasting just a few seconds—where you stare blankly and don't respond to your surroundings. The \"intractable\" part means your seizures are not well-controlled by medication, and \"with status epilepticus\" indicates you've experienced a serious emergency situation where seizures happen repeatedly without stopping, which requires immediate medical attention. This is a rare form of epilepsy that usually starts in childhood and requires aggressive treatment to prevent dangerous complications.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and epilepsy specialists use this code when managing patients with drug-resistant absence seizures, particularly when documenting status epilepticus events that required emergency department or ICU-level care. This code appears on EOB statements less frequently than other seizure diagnoses but is important when coding for hospital admissions, emergency visits, or specialist consultations related to uncontrolled absence seizures.",
