    "longDesc": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, not intractable, without status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME) is a type of seizure disorder that typically starts in early adolescence and causes sudden, involuntary jerking movements, especially in the arms and hands—often noticed first thing in the morning or when tired. This code indicates you have JME that is currently controlled by medication (not intractable, meaning your seizures respond to treatment) and you are not currently experiencing status epilepticus, which is a dangerous condition of prolonged or repeated seizures. Most people with JME can manage their condition well with anti-seizure medications.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and pediatric neurologists primarily diagnose and code JME during initial evaluation and ongoing seizure management. This code commonly appears on patient EOBs for office visits, EEG studies, and medication management related to seizure control. It may also be used by primary care physicians who refer patients to neurology or who manage the condition under specialist guidance.",
