    "plainEnglish": "Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is a type of seizure disorder that typically starts in childhood or early adolescence and causes sudden, involuntary jerking movements of the arms, legs, or torso, especially upon waking. The 'intractable' part means the seizures are difficult to control with anti-seizure medications, and 'status epilepticus' indicates the patient experienced a prolonged or repeated seizure that requires emergency intervention. This is a serious neurological condition requiring close medical management and potentially multiple medications or other treatments to prevent complications.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and epilepsy specialists use this code primarily in hospital settings, emergency departments, and specialized epilepsy clinics when documenting acute seizure episodes or long-term management of drug-resistant epilepsy. This code is less common on routine EOB statements but may appear when patients are hospitalized for status epilepticus or receive specialized epilepsy care and testing.",
