    "longDesc": "Other epilepsy, not intractable, with status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis means you have epilepsy that is causing a prolonged or repeated seizure emergency called status epilepticus, but your epilepsy is otherwise controllable with medication. Status epilepticus is a serious condition where seizures happen back-to-back without the person fully waking up between them, which requires immediate emergency treatment. Unlike some forms of epilepsy that don't respond well to drugs, your epilepsy can generally be managed with seizure medications once this acute crisis is treated.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, emergency medicine physicians, and intensive care specialists use this code when documenting acute seizure clusters or prolonged seizures in patients with known epilepsy. This code commonly appears on EOB statements for emergency department visits, hospital admissions, and ICU care related to seizure emergencies.",
