    "longDesc": "Epileptic spasms, intractable, without status epilepticus",
    "plainEnglish": "You have a severe form of epilepsy called infantile spasms (or West syndrome) where your muscles suddenly contract in brief, violent jerking movements, and this condition is not responding well to anti-seizure medications. These spasms are considered \"intractable,\" meaning doctors have tried multiple medications without adequate control, though you are not currently in a prolonged, continuous seizure state (status epilepticus). Patients typically experience clusters of these sudden, forceful muscle contractions—often involving the head, neck, and limbs—that can happen many times per day.",
