    "rawCode": "G43409",
    "shortDesc": "Hemiplegic migraine, not intractable, w/o status migrainosus",
    "longDesc": "Hemiplegic migraine, not intractable, without status migrainosus",
    "plainEnglish": "Hemiplegic migraine is a rare and serious type of migraine headache where you experience temporary weakness or paralysis on one side of your body (usually an arm or leg) along with the headache. The weakness typically happens before or during the migraine attack and goes away as the headache improves. This code indicates your migraine is responding to treatment (not intractable) and you're not currently in a prolonged migraine state.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and primary care physicians use this code when diagnosing and treating hemiplegic migraines. It commonly appears on patient EOBs when documenting migraine-related office visits, emergency department evaluations, or neurological consultations, though it's relatively uncommon compared to other migraine types.",
