    "rawCode": "G43411",
    "shortDesc": "Hemiplegic migraine, intractable, with status migrainosus",
    "longDesc": "Hemiplegic migraine, intractable, with status migrainosus",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes a severe form of hemiplegic migraine—a rare type of migraine headache that causes temporary weakness or paralysis on one side of your body, along with the usual migraine symptoms like intense head pain and nausea. The 'intractable' part means your migraine isn't responding well to standard treatment, and 'status migrainosus' means the headache and symptoms have lasted much longer than typical (usually 72 hours or more), creating a prolonged medical emergency that requires intensive care. These symptoms can mimic a stroke, which is why hemiplegic migraine is taken very seriously by doctors.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and emergency medicine physicians use this code when treating patients experiencing prolonged, treatment-resistant hemiplegic migraines requiring hospitalization or urgent intervention. This code commonly appears on EOB statements for patients admitted to the hospital or undergoing emergency department care for severe, uncontrolled migraine symptoms.",
