    "longDesc": "Hemiplegic migraine, intractable, without status migrainosus",
    "plainEnglish": "You have a severe type of migraine called hemiplegic migraine, which causes temporary paralysis or weakness on one side of your body during the attack—along with the typical migraine headache, nausea, and sensitivity to light. The word 'intractable' means your migraines are difficult to treat and don't respond well to standard migraine medications, making them hard to control. This particular code indicates you're experiencing these severe, treatment-resistant migraines but are not currently in a prolonged migraine state (called 'status migrainosus').",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and headache specialists use this code when diagnosing and treating patients with refractory hemiplegic migraines. This code appears on EOB statements for patients seeking specialist care, imaging studies, or preventive medication management for difficult-to-treat migraines with neurological symptoms.",
