    "shortDesc": "Episodic cluster headache, not intractable",
    "longDesc": "Episodic cluster headache, not intractable",
    "plainEnglish": "A cluster headache is a severe, recurring headache that happens in groups or cycles—you might have several intense headaches over weeks or months, then experience pain-free periods lasting months or years. This code specifically means you have episodic cluster headaches (the type that comes and goes in cycles rather than continuously), and that your headaches are manageable with current treatment. Cluster headaches typically cause excruciating pain on one side of the head, often around the eye, and may be accompanied by symptoms like tearing, redness, or nasal congestion.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and headache specialists primarily use this code when diagnosing and treating patients with cluster headaches. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients seeking treatment at headache clinics or neurology offices, particularly when documenting the type of headache for insurance purposes and treatment planning.",
    "billable": true,
    "isNew": false,
    "isRevised": false,
