    "rawCode": "G4451",
    "shortDesc": "Hemicrania continua",
    "longDesc": "Hemicrania continua",
    "plainEnglish": "Hemicrania continua is a rare headache disorder where you experience a constant, one-sided headache that persists for months or years without stopping. Unlike migraines that come and go, this headache is always present, though it may vary in intensity throughout the day, and it's typically moderate in severity. Patients often experience brief periods of sharp, stabbing pain on top of the constant ache, sometimes accompanied by symptoms like watery eyes, nasal congestion, or drooping of the eyelid on the affected side.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and headache specialists primarily diagnose and treat hemicrania continua, as it requires specialized knowledge to distinguish it from other chronic headache conditions. This code appears less frequently on patient EOB statements since it's a rare diagnosis, but when it does appear, it's usually associated with neurology visits, imaging studies, or specialized headache clinic consultations.",
