    "shortDesc": "New daily persistent headache (NDPH)",
    "longDesc": "New daily persistent headache (NDPH)",
    "plainEnglish": "New daily persistent headache (NDPH) is a type of headache that starts suddenly and then occurs almost every day without stopping. Unlike migraines or tension headaches that come and go, patients with NDPH experience a continuous or nearly constant headache that began on a specific day they can usually remember. The pain is often mild to moderate and may feel like a pressure or tightness around the head.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and headache specialists primarily use this code when evaluating patients with daily, unrelenting headaches that have persisted for extended periods. This code is commonly documented in neurology clinics and pain management centers, and may appear on patient EOB statements when the headache disorder is the primary reason for the office visit or evaluation.",
