    "shortDesc": "Hypnic headache",
    "longDesc": "Hypnic headache",
    "plainEnglish": "A hypnic headache is a rare type of headache that happens specifically when you're sleeping or trying to sleep—it typically wakes you up in the middle of the night or early morning. The pain is usually moderate, feels like a dull throb, and lasts anywhere from 15 minutes to a few hours, though the exact cause isn't well understood. It's more common in older adults and tends to happen regularly at similar times each night.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and primary care physicians diagnose and manage hypnic headaches, often after ruling out other sleep-related or serious conditions. This code appears less frequently on patient EOBs compared to common headache types, but may show up when a patient has had sleep studies or specialized neurology evaluation for nocturnal headaches.",
