    "plainEnglish": "A primary cough headache is a sudden, severe head pain triggered specifically by coughing, sneezing, straining, or other activities that increase pressure in your head and chest. This type of headache occurs without an underlying brain or structural problem causing it—it's simply the way your body reacts to that sudden pressure change. The pain typically comes on very quickly during or right after the triggering activity and usually goes away within seconds to minutes.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and primary care physicians diagnose and manage primary cough headaches when patients report this specific symptom pattern. This code appears in medical records and may show on EOB statements when patients seek evaluation for sudden severe headaches triggered by coughing or straining, though it's a relatively uncommon diagnosis.",
