    "plainEnglish": "A cervicogenic headache is a type of head pain that originates from a problem in your neck rather than your brain itself. This can happen when muscles, nerves, or joints in your neck are injured, strained, or irritated—perhaps from poor posture, whiplash, arthritis, or a pinched nerve—and that problem radiates up to cause headache pain, often on one side of the head or at the back of the head. You might also notice neck stiffness, shoulder pain, or reduced neck movement along with the headache.",
