    "shortDesc": "Multiple and bilateral precerebral artery syndromes",
    "longDesc": "Multiple and bilateral precerebral artery syndromes",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis describes a condition where blood flow to the brain is partially blocked or reduced in multiple blood vessels on both sides of the neck (the carotid and vertebral arteries that carry blood to the brain). Patients with this condition typically experience temporary neurological symptoms like weakness, numbness, vision changes, or difficulty speaking, which may come and go as blood flow fluctuates. These episodes are warning signs that a stroke could occur if the blocked vessels are not treated.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, vascular neurologists, and stroke specialists use this code when diagnosing patients with transient ischemic attacks (TIAs) or narrowing in multiple brain blood vessels affecting both sides of the neck. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing imaging studies (ultrasound, MRI, or angiography) and specialist consultations related to stroke prevention.",
