    "shortDesc": "Transient global amnesia",
    "longDesc": "Transient global amnesia",
    "plainEnglish": "Transient global amnesia is a sudden, temporary loss of memory that comes on without warning and typically lasts a few hours. During an episode, a person cannot form new memories and may not remember recent events, though they generally retain their identity and long-term memories, and the condition resolves completely on its own without any lasting damage to the brain.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and emergency medicine physicians typically diagnose and document this code, often in hospital or urgent care settings when patients present with acute memory loss. While it's a significant medical event, it appears less frequently on routine patient EOB statements since episodes are self-limited and don't usually require ongoing treatment or billing codes across multiple visits.",
