    "shortDesc": "Middle cerebral artery syndrome",
    "longDesc": "Middle cerebral artery syndrome",
    "plainEnglish": "Middle cerebral artery syndrome occurs when blood flow is blocked or severely reduced in the middle cerebral artery, one of the major blood vessels that supplies blood to the brain. This blockage can happen suddenly due to a blood clot or narrowed vessel, and depending on which part of the brain is affected, you may experience weakness or paralysis on one side of your body, difficulty speaking, vision problems, or loss of sensation. The severity and specific symptoms depend on how much of the brain is deprived of oxygen and how quickly blood flow is restored.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, stroke specialists (neurointerventionalists), emergency medicine physicians, and hospitalists use this code when diagnosing acute stroke or transient ischemic attacks affecting the middle cerebral artery distribution. This code commonly appears on patient EOB statements as it documents a serious medical event requiring urgent hospitalization and imaging studies.",
