    "shortDesc": "Anterior cerebral artery syndrome",
    "longDesc": "Anterior cerebral artery syndrome",
    "plainEnglish": "Anterior cerebral artery syndrome occurs when blood flow is blocked or severely reduced in the anterior cerebral artery, one of the main blood vessels that supplies the front and inner portions of the brain. This can happen due to a blood clot, narrowing of the vessel, or other vascular problems, and typically causes a stroke. Depending on which part of the brain is affected, a patient might experience weakness or paralysis on one side of the body (especially the legs), difficulty with balance and coordination, changes in personality or behavior, or problems with urinary control.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, stroke specialists, emergency medicine physicians, and neurosurgeons use this code when diagnosing and treating acute stroke patients. This code commonly appears on hospital bills and EOB statements following a stroke event, often alongside imaging and emergency department claims.",
