    "shortDesc": "Circadian rhythm sleep disorder, jet lag type",
    "longDesc": "Circadian rhythm sleep disorder, jet lag type",
    "plainEnglish": "Jet lag is a temporary sleep disorder that occurs when you travel across multiple time zones quickly, causing your internal body clock to be out of sync with the local time. Your brain's natural sleep-wake cycle (circadian rhythm) takes time to adjust to the new time zone, so you might experience severe fatigue, difficulty falling asleep, waking up too early, or feeling alert at odd hours until your body adapts. This condition typically resolves within a few days to a week as your body clock gradually realigns with your new location.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists, travel medicine doctors, and primary care physicians may document this code when patients report sleep disturbances following rapid air travel across time zones. This code is common on EOB statements for patients who sought medical evaluation or treatment for jet lag symptoms, particularly after long international flights.",
