    "shortDesc": "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult) (pediatric)",
    "longDesc": "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult) (pediatric)",
    "plainEnglish": "Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a sleep disorder where your airway repeatedly collapses or becomes blocked during sleep, causing you to stop breathing for brief periods throughout the night. These breathing pauses can happen dozens of times per hour and may lower the oxygen levels in your blood, leading to gasping awake, loud snoring, daytime fatigue, and morning headaches. The blockage usually happens because the muscles in the back of your throat relax too much during sleep, though extra weight, enlarged tonsils, or structural issues in the nose or jaw can increase your risk.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists, pulmonologists, otolaryngologists (ENTs), primary care physicians, and cardiologists commonly diagnose and manage OSA. This code frequently appears on patient EOBs for sleep studies, CPAP machines, and related treatments, making it one of the most commonly billed sleep disorder codes in outpatient and home health settings.",
