    "code": "G47.411",
    "rawCode": "G47411",
    "shortDesc": "Narcolepsy with cataplexy",
    "longDesc": "Narcolepsy with cataplexy",
    "plainEnglish": "Narcolepsy with cataplexy is a sleep disorder where a person experiences uncontrollable daytime drowsiness and sudden episodes of muscle weakness or paralysis, usually triggered by strong emotions like laughter, anger, or surprise. During a cataplexy attack, your muscles may go slack for a few seconds to minutes while you remain conscious and aware—you might slur your words, droop your head, or collapse entirely. This condition is caused by a deficiency of a brain chemical called hypocretin, which normally helps regulate sleep-wake cycles and muscle tone.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists, neurologists, and primary care physicians diagnose and manage narcolepsy with cataplexy. This code appears on EOB statements when patients receive sleep studies, neurology consultations, or medication management for this chronic neurological condition.",
    "billable": true,
    "isNew": false,
    "isRevised": false,
