    "plainEnglish": "Narcolepsy is a chronic neurological disorder that causes your brain to struggle with controlling sleep-wake cycles, leading to excessive daytime sleepiness, sudden and irresistible sleep attacks, and hallucinations or sleep paralysis. In this type of narcolepsy, you experience these disruptive sleep symptoms without cataplexy—which means you don't have the sudden loss of muscle tone or strength that some narcolepsy patients experience when they're awake (such as your knees buckling or head drooping when laughing or startled).",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists, neurologists, and primary care physicians diagnose and manage narcolepsy, often with sleep studies like polysomnography and the multiple sleep latency test. This code commonly appears on EOB statements for patients seeking evaluation, diagnosis, and ongoing treatment of this chronic sleep disorder.",
