    "rawCode": "G4751",
    "shortDesc": "Confusional arousals",
    "longDesc": "Confusional arousals",
    "plainEnglish": "Confusional arousals are episodes where you wake up but feel extremely confused and disoriented for a few minutes to over an hour—you might not know where you are, what time it is, or even recognize people around you. These episodes typically happen during the deeper stages of sleep and are more common in children, though they can occur in adults; you may appear awake and even move around or speak, but your brain hasn't fully \"switched on\" yet. The confusion eventually clears on its own without any treatment needed, though the episodes can be frightening for family members who witness them.",
    "whoUses": "Sleep medicine specialists and neurologists diagnose and manage confusional arousals, often during sleep studies or clinical evaluations for parasomnia disorders. This code appears less frequently on patient EOBs as it's typically documented during specialty sleep consultations rather than routine primary care visits, though it may be noted when insurance covers sleep disorder evaluations.",
