    "plainEnglish": "Sleep paralysis is a temporary inability to move or speak that happens when you're falling asleep or waking up, even though your mind is alert and aware. In recurrent isolated sleep paralysis, these episodes occur repeatedly over time without being caused by another sleep disorder like narcolepsy. During an episode, you might feel chest pressure, see or hear things that aren't there, or sense a threatening presence in the room, which can be frightening—but the paralysis itself is harmless and typically lasts from seconds to a few minutes.",
