    "rawCode": "G501",
    "shortDesc": "Atypical facial pain",
    "longDesc": "Atypical facial pain",
    "plainEnglish": "Atypical facial pain is a chronic pain condition affecting the face that doesn't follow the typical pattern of other well-known facial pain disorders like trigeminal neuralgia. This pain is typically dull, aching, or burning and may feel like it's on one side of the face, upper jaw, or near the teeth, but medical tests usually don't reveal an obvious cause like a nerve problem or infection. Patients often experience ongoing discomfort that can significantly affect their quality of life and may require evaluation by multiple specialists to rule out other conditions.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, pain management specialists, and dentists commonly use this code when evaluating patients with unexplained facial pain. It appears on patient EOB statements relatively infrequently, as it's typically reserved for cases where other specific facial pain diagnoses have been excluded.",
