    "shortDesc": "Disorder of trigeminal nerve, unspecified",
    "longDesc": "Disorder of trigeminal nerve, unspecified",
    "plainEnglish": "This code indicates a problem with your trigeminal nerve—one of the major nerves in your face that controls sensation and chewing—but the specific type of problem hasn't been identified or detailed. The trigeminal nerve has three branches (hence the name \"tri\") that spread across your face, and when it's not functioning properly, you might experience facial pain, numbness, tingling, or weakness in your jaw or facial muscles. Without more specific information, this code represents a general trigeminal nerve disorder that requires further investigation or clarification to determine the exact cause and treatment.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, neurosurgeons, and primary care physicians use this code when a patient has trigeminal nerve symptoms but the underlying condition hasn't been precisely diagnosed yet (such as trigeminal neuralgia, infection, or nerve compression). This is a less common code on EOBs since providers typically determine a more specific diagnosis before billing; it may appear if testing is still pending or if the condition remains unclear after initial evaluation.",
