    "plainEnglish": "Bell's palsy is a condition where the nerve that controls the muscles on one side of your face suddenly becomes inflamed or swollen, causing weakness or paralysis of those facial muscles. This results in a drooping appearance on the affected side of your face, difficulty closing your eye, and trouble smiling or moving that side of your mouth—though it's usually temporary and often improves on its own over weeks to months.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, primary care physicians, and otolaryngologists (ENT specialists) commonly diagnose and manage Bell's palsy. This code appears regularly on patient EOB statements when someone seeks treatment for sudden facial weakness, as it's a relatively common acute condition affecting roughly 1 in 5,000 people annually.",
