    "shortDesc": "Geniculate ganglionitis",
    "longDesc": "Geniculate ganglionitis",
    "plainEnglish": "Geniculate ganglionitis is an inflammation of the geniculate ganglion, a nerve bundle in your skull that contains nerve cells related to facial sensation and taste. This condition causes pain, swelling, and irritation in this small nerve cluster, often resulting in facial pain, ear pain, or changes in taste sensation. The inflammation can occur after a viral infection (especially herpes zoster) or sometimes without a clear cause, and patients may experience sharp pain around the ear or temple area.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, otolaryngologists (ENT specialists), and infectious disease doctors use this code when diagnosing nerve inflammation, particularly in cases of suspected Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus). While it appears in medical records and hospital coding, G51.1 is less common on patient EOB statements because it typically requires specialist evaluation rather than routine primary care billing.",
