    "shortDesc": "Clonic hemifacial spasm, right",
    "longDesc": "Clonic hemifacial spasm, right",
    "plainEnglish": "Hemifacial spasm is an involuntary twitching or spasm that affects the muscles on one side of your face, in this case the right side. The 'clonic' part means the spasms involve repeated, rhythmic contractions of the facial muscles rather than a sustained stiffness. This condition is typically caused by a blood vessel pressing on the facial nerve, and you would experience repetitive, uncontrollable facial movements that can be bothersome or socially embarrassing, though the condition is not life-threatening.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and neurosurgeons commonly diagnose and treat hemifacial spasm, and the code may also be used by primary care physicians or otolaryngologists. This diagnosis frequently appears on patient EOB statements as it is a recognized billable condition for imaging studies (MRI), specialist consultations, and treatments such as botulinum toxin injections.",
