    "shortDesc": "Disorders of glossopharyngeal nerve",
    "longDesc": "Disorders of glossopharyngeal nerve",
    "plainEnglish": "The glossopharyngeal nerve is a cranial nerve that controls sensation and movement in your throat, including taste on the back of your tongue and swallowing. When this nerve malfunctions, you may experience pain in the throat or ear, difficulty swallowing, loss of taste, or weakness in throat muscles. This can happen due to inflammation, infection, injury, or compression of the nerve.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, otolaryngologists (ENT doctors), and speech-language pathologists diagnose and treat glossopharyngeal nerve disorders. This code appears on EOBs when patients undergo nerve testing, imaging studies, or treatment for throat-related neurological symptoms.",
