    "rawCode": "G523",
    "shortDesc": "Disorders of hypoglossal nerve",
    "longDesc": "Disorders of hypoglossal nerve",
    "plainEnglish": "The hypoglossal nerve controls the muscles of your tongue, so damage to this nerve causes weakness or paralysis of the tongue on one or both sides. This can make it difficult to move your tongue properly when speaking, eating, or swallowing, and you might notice your tongue feels numb or droops to one side. Common causes include stroke, tumors, nerve compression, or trauma to the neck or skull.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, stroke specialists, head and neck surgeons, and otolaryngologists use this code when diagnosing tongue weakness or paralysis from hypoglossal nerve damage. This code is moderately common on patient EOBs when tongue dysfunction is evaluated or treated, particularly following stroke or after neck surgery.",
