    "longDesc": "Cranial nerve disorder, unspecified",
    "plainEnglish": "This code indicates that you have a problem with one or more of the cranial nerves (the 12 pairs of nerves in your head and neck that control functions like vision, hearing, taste, facial movement, and swallowing), but your doctor hasn't identified the specific cranial nerve affected or the exact cause. You might be experiencing symptoms like facial weakness, drooping eyelids, difficulty chewing or swallowing, hearing loss, or balance problems, depending on which nerve is involved.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, primary care physicians, and emergency medicine doctors use this code when a patient presents with symptoms suggesting cranial nerve involvement but the specific nerve disorder hasn't been diagnosed yet. It commonly appears on EOBs as a temporary or initial diagnosis code during the diagnostic workup phase, often before more specific testing (like imaging or electromyography) clarifies the exact condition.",
