    "shortDesc": "Neuralgic amyotrophy",
    "longDesc": "Neuralgic amyotrophy",
    "plainEnglish": "Neuralgic amyotrophy is a condition where the nerves in your shoulder and upper arm become inflamed, causing sudden severe pain followed by muscle weakness and wasting (shrinking) in that area. The pain typically comes on suddenly and can be quite intense, but it usually improves over time as the nerve inflammation resolves, though recovery of muscle strength may take weeks to months. This condition can happen on its own or follow an injury, illness, or vaccination, and it primarily affects the nerves that control the shoulder and arm muscles.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, primary care physicians, and orthopedic specialists use this code when diagnosing shoulder and upper arm pain with associated muscle weakness. It appears on patient EOBs when someone is being evaluated or treated for this nerve inflammation condition, typically during imaging studies, nerve conduction tests, or follow-up care visits.",
    "billable": true,
    "isNew": false,
    "isRevised": false,
