    "longDesc": "Phantom limb syndrome with pain",
    "plainEnglish": "Phantom limb syndrome with pain is a condition where a person experiences pain in a limb that has been amputated or removed. Even though the limb is no longer physically present, the brain continues to send pain signals as if the missing limb still exists. This can feel like burning, cramping, shooting, or aching sensations in the area where the amputated limb used to be.",
    "whoUses": "Orthopedic surgeons, neurologists, pain management specialists, and prosthetists commonly use this code when treating post-amputation patients experiencing phantom pain. This diagnosis frequently appears on EOB statements for patients receiving rehabilitation, pain management treatment, or prosthetic fitting services after amputation surgery.",
