    "shortDesc": "Phantom limb syndrome without pain",
    "longDesc": "Phantom limb syndrome without pain",
    "plainEnglish": "Phantom limb syndrome is a condition where a person who has had a limb amputated continues to feel sensations from that missing limb, even though it's no longer there. In this case, you're experiencing these phantom sensations—such as tingling, pressure, or awareness of the limb's position—but without accompanying pain. These sensations occur because your brain still has neural pathways connected to the amputated limb, and it continues to send signals as if the limb were still present.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, orthopedic surgeons, physiatrists (rehabilitation medicine specialists), and pain management specialists use this code. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients recovering from amputation who report phantom sensations during follow-up care or rehabilitation visits.",
