    "longDesc": "Lesion of ulnar nerve, unspecified upper limb",
    "plainEnglish": "You have damage or injury to the ulnar nerve in one of your arms, but the specific type or location of the damage hasn't been fully identified yet. The ulnar nerve runs from your neck down through your arm to your hand and controls sensation and movement in part of your forearm and hand—when it's injured, you might feel numbness, tingling, weakness, or pain in your pinky finger and half of your ring finger. This code is used when doctors know there's a problem with the ulnar nerve but haven't yet determined the exact cause (like from compression, trauma, or disease).",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, orthopedic surgeons, and primary care physicians use this code when evaluating patients with symptoms of ulnar nerve damage such as numbness, weakness, or hand pain. It commonly appears on EOB statements for office visits, nerve conduction studies, or imaging ordered to investigate arm or hand symptoms.",
