    "shortDesc": "Meralgia paresthetica, left lower limb",
    "longDesc": "Meralgia paresthetica, left lower limb",
    "plainEnglish": "Meralgia paresthetica is a nerve pinching condition that affects the left thigh, causing numbness, tingling, or burning sensations along the outer part of your leg. This happens when the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve—which runs under or through a ligament in your groin—gets compressed, often due to tight clothing, weight gain, or prolonged pressure on that area. Most people with this condition experience uncomfortable sensations but no actual weakness, and it typically improves with conservative treatment like loosening tight waistbands or losing weight.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, primary care physicians, and orthopedic specialists commonly diagnose and manage meralgia paresthetica. This code frequently appears on patient EOBs when someone seeks care for unexplained thigh pain or abnormal sensations and receives a formal diagnosis after clinical evaluation or imaging studies.",
