    "shortDesc": "Guillain-Barre syndrome",
    "longDesc": "Guillain-Barre syndrome",
    "plainEnglish": "Guillain-Barré syndrome is a rare, serious autoimmune condition where your body's immune system mistakenly attacks the nerves that control your muscles, causing weakness and sometimes paralysis that typically starts in your legs and moves upward. This condition often develops after a viral or bacterial infection and can develop over hours to days, sometimes requiring hospitalization and respiratory support if the breathing muscles become affected. Symptoms may include tingling sensations, muscle weakness, difficulty walking, and in severe cases, temporary inability to move or breathe without assistance.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and critical care physicians most commonly diagnose and treat Guillain-Barré syndrome, often in emergency or intensive care settings. This code frequently appears on EOB statements as it is a serious, often hospitalized condition requiring significant medical intervention and ongoing specialty care.",
