    "shortDesc": "Serum neuropathy",
    "longDesc": "Serum neuropathy",
    "plainEnglish": "Serum neuropathy is nerve damage caused by a reaction to a serum or antiserum—typically after receiving an injection of antibodies, antivenom, or other blood-derived products. Your immune system mistakenly attacks the nerves, causing symptoms like weakness, numbness, tingling, or pain, usually in the arms and legs, which may develop within days to weeks after the injection.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and infectious disease specialists use this code when diagnosing nerve damage following serum administration (such as antiserum for snake bites, rabies post-exposure prophylaxis, or tetanus immunoglobulin). This diagnosis appears on patient EOBs when serum-related neuropathy complications are documented and billed.",
