    "shortDesc": "Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuritis",
    "longDesc": "Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuritis",
    "plainEnglish": "Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuritis (CIDP) is a rare neurological condition where your immune system gradually damages the protective coating (myelin) around nerves in your limbs, causing progressive weakness and impaired function over weeks to months. This damage disrupts how nerves communicate with muscles, leading to symptoms like weakness, tingling, numbness, and difficulty walking or using your hands that slowly get worse over time. Unlike acute cases that develop suddenly, CIDP develops gradually and can become disabling if not treated with immunosuppressive therapy.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and neuromuscular specialists use this code when diagnosing and managing CIDP, often after EMG/NCS testing confirms demyelination. This code commonly appears on patient EOB statements because CIDP typically requires ongoing specialty care, regular nerve testing, and expensive immunosuppressive treatments like IVIG or corticosteroids.",
