    "plainEnglish": "Multifocal motor neuropathy is a rare condition where the nerves that control muscle movement become damaged in multiple different areas of your body, causing weakness and loss of function primarily in your arms and hands. This autoimmune condition occurs when your body's immune system mistakenly attacks the protective covering of these nerves, leading to progressive weakness, muscle twitching, and cramping that typically worsens over time. Patients often experience difficulty with fine motor tasks like writing or buttoning clothes, and the condition usually affects one side of the body more than the other.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and neuromuscular specialists primarily diagnose and manage multifocal motor neuropathy, often using electrophysiological testing (EMG/NCS) to confirm the diagnosis. This code appears less frequently on routine EOB statements as it's a specialized neurological diagnosis, but patients receiving ongoing neurology care, immunoglobulin treatments, or immunosuppressive therapy will see it documented in their medical records.",
