    "longDesc": "Drug-induced polyneuropathy",
    "plainEnglish": "This diagnosis means you have nerve damage affecting multiple nerves in your body that was caused by a medication you took. Certain drugs—including chemotherapy drugs, some antibiotics, antiretroviral medications for HIV, and other treatments—can damage the protective coating of nerves or interfere with how they work, leading to symptoms like tingling, numbness, weakness, or pain, usually starting in your hands and feet. Once your doctor identifies which medication is causing the problem, they may adjust your dose, switch you to a different drug, or stop the medication to prevent further nerve damage.",
    "whoUses": "Oncologists, neurologists, infectious disease specialists, and primary care physicians use this code when documenting nerve damage linked to medications their patients are taking. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing chemotherapy or long-term drug therapy, as insurers need to track medication-related complications for coverage and safety monitoring purposes.",
