    "plainEnglish": "Myasthenia gravis is a condition where your immune system attacks the connections between your nerves and muscles, causing muscle weakness that gets worse with activity and improves with rest. When you have an acute exacerbation, it means your symptoms have suddenly gotten much worse, such as severe weakness in your eyes, face, or breathing muscles—this is a serious flare-up that often requires urgent medical attention and possibly hospitalization.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, neuromuscular specialists, and emergency medicine physicians use this code when a patient with myasthenia gravis experiences a sudden worsening of symptoms. This code commonly appears on EOB statements for patients admitted to the hospital or treated in emergency departments during crisis episodes, as exacerbations often require IV immunoglobulin, plasma exchange, or other intensive treatments.",
