    "plainEnglish": "Lambert-Eaton syndrome (LES) is a rare autoimmune condition where your immune system attacks nerve endings, preventing them from properly releasing a chemical messenger called acetylcholine needed for muscle movement. This causes muscle weakness that typically starts in the legs and can spread upward, along with dry mouth and difficulty with eye focusing. The code G70.81 specifically indicates that this nerve condition has developed as a complication of another disease that the patient has (which is documented separately in their medical record).",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and oncologists primarily use this code, as Lambert-Eaton syndrome is often associated with small cell lung cancer or other underlying diseases. It may appear on EOBs when patients are being evaluated for unexplained muscle weakness or when the underlying malignancy is being treated, though the code itself is less commonly seen on routine patient statements compared to more prevalent neurological diagnoses.",
    "billable": true,
