    "shortDesc": "Myotonia congenita",
    "longDesc": "Myotonia congenita",
    "plainEnglish": "Myotonia congenita is a genetic muscle disorder present from birth where your muscles have difficulty relaxing after contraction, causing temporary stiffness or rigidity. When you try to move—like opening your fist or standing up—your muscles stay tense longer than they should, which gradually improves with repeated use. This happens because of a defect in how chloride channels function in muscle cells, and while it can be uncomfortable and limit physical activity, it typically doesn't get progressively worse or cause muscle breakdown over time.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and genetic specialists diagnose and manage myotonia congenita, often using electromyography (EMG) testing to confirm the condition. This code appears on EOBs when patients undergo diagnostic testing, genetic counseling, or specialist visits for evaluation of muscle stiffness or inherited neuromuscular conditions.",
