    "longDesc": "Drug induced myotonia",
    "plainEnglish": "Drug-induced myotonia is a muscle condition where your muscles become stiff and have difficulty relaxing after use, and this stiffness is caused by a medication you're taking rather than by an inherited genetic disorder. You might notice that after gripping something, closing your eyes forcefully, or moving a muscle, it takes longer than normal for that muscle to relax and become loose again. Common medications that can cause this include certain statins (cholesterol drugs), anesthetics, or other drugs—and the condition typically improves once the medication is stopped or changed.",
