    "code": "G72.0",
    "rawCode": "G720",
    "shortDesc": "Drug-induced myopathy",
    "longDesc": "Drug-induced myopathy",
    "plainEnglish": "Drug-induced myopathy means that certain medications you're taking have damaged or weakened your muscles, making them painful, tired, or difficult to use. This condition can develop as a side effect from various drugs—including cholesterol-lowering medications (statins), corticosteroids, certain antibiotics, and some anti-cancer treatments—and typically improves once the offending medication is stopped or the dose is reduced. You might notice muscle pain, weakness, soreness, or difficulty with activities like climbing stairs or lifting objects.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, rheumatologists, and primary care physicians use this code when evaluating muscle weakness or pain potentially linked to medication use. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing diagnostic workup (blood tests, muscle biopsies, EMG studies) or medication management adjustments related to suspected medication side effects.",
