    "code": "G72.1",
    "rawCode": "G721",
    "shortDesc": "Alcoholic myopathy",
    "longDesc": "Alcoholic myopathy",
    "plainEnglish": "Alcoholic myopathy is muscle disease caused by heavy alcohol consumption, where the muscles become weak, painful, or damaged over time. When someone drinks excessively, alcohol can directly harm muscle fibers and interfere with the body's ability to absorb nutrients needed for muscle health, leading to symptoms like weakness, soreness, cramping, or difficulty with physical activities. This condition can improve if someone stops drinking and receives proper nutrition, but continued alcohol use will make it progressively worse.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists, primary care physicians, and addiction medicine specialists use this code when diagnosing muscle weakness or pain related to alcohol abuse. It commonly appears in hospital records and specialist referrals but is less frequently seen on patient EOBs unless the patient is being specifically treated for alcohol-related complications with follow-up muscle testing or rehabilitation.",
