    "rawCode": "G723",
    "shortDesc": "Periodic paralysis",
    "longDesc": "Periodic paralysis",
    "plainEnglish": "Periodic paralysis is a rare genetic muscle disorder where a person experiences sudden, temporary episodes of muscle weakness or complete inability to move, often triggered by rest after exercise, certain foods (like high carbohydrates), temperature changes, or stress. During an attack, muscles may feel completely limp and unresponsive for hours to days, though sensation and consciousness remain normal—the person is awake and aware but cannot move. Between episodes, most people return to normal muscle strength, though repeated attacks over years can sometimes cause permanent weakness.",
    "whoUses": "Neurologists and geneticists diagnose and manage periodic paralysis, often with support from muscle disease specialists (myologists). This code appears on EOB statements when patients undergo diagnostic testing (genetic testing, electrolyte panels, EMG studies) or follow-up care for this condition, though it is relatively uncommon in primary care settings.",
