    "shortDesc": "Critical illness myopathy",
    "longDesc": "Critical illness myopathy",
    "plainEnglish": "Critical illness myopathy is a condition where the muscles become severely weakened during or after a serious illness, typically in patients who have been very sick and require intensive care. This weakness happens because the critical illness itself—such as sepsis, respiratory failure, or prolonged organ dysfunction—damages the muscle fibers, making it difficult for patients to move, breathe on their own, or recover their strength even after the acute illness improves. Patients may notice they have extreme difficulty moving their limbs, difficulty breathing, or take much longer to regain strength and function during recovery.",
    "whoUses": "Intensivists, critical care physicians, and neurologists diagnose and manage this condition in hospitalized patients, particularly in ICU settings. This code appears less frequently on routine patient EOBs but is more common in hospital discharge summaries and during post-ICU follow-up visits or rehabilitation admissions.",
