    "longDesc": "Spastic quadriplegic cerebral palsy",
    "plainEnglish": "Spastic quadriplegic cerebral palsy is a permanent movement disorder caused by brain damage that occurred before, during, or shortly after birth, affecting all four limbs and trunk. People with this condition have muscles that are abnormally stiff and tight (spastic), making it difficult or impossible to move their arms, legs, and core muscles with normal control. Depending on severity, someone with this diagnosis may have significant physical limitations ranging from difficulty with coordination to complete paralysis, and many individuals also experience speech, vision, or learning challenges.",
    "whoUses": "Pediatric neurologists, physiatrists (rehabilitation medicine doctors), orthopedic surgeons, physical medicine and rehabilitation specialists, and developmental pediatricians typically use this code. While it appears on patient records and insurance explanations of benefits, it is primarily used in clinical documentation, therapy planning, and disability determinations rather than as a common diagnosis on routine EOB statements.",
