    "shortDesc": "Chronic post-thoracotomy pain",
    "longDesc": "Chronic post-thoracotomy pain",
    "plainEnglish": "This code describes ongoing pain that develops after chest surgery (thoracotomy), where the surgeon makes an incision through the chest wall to access the lungs or heart. Even after the surgical wound has healed, some patients experience persistent pain in the chest area that lasts beyond the normal recovery period. This type of chronic pain can occur along the incision site, in the muscles between the ribs, or deeper in the chest, and may be triggered by movement, breathing, or activity.",
    "whoUses": "Thoracic surgeons, pain management specialists, and cardiothoracic surgeons use this code to document persistent pain complaints in patients recovering from chest surgery. It commonly appears on EOB statements for patients undergoing pain treatment or physical therapy months or years after thoracic procedures like lung resection, heart surgery, or esophageal surgery.",
